==English==

===Etymology===
from the practice of ''[[w:Billboard (magazine)|Billboard]]'' magazine of putting a [[bullet]] sign in front of some chart entries

In the [[1960s]] and [[1970s]] on [[w:Billboard Hot 100|Billboard Hot 100]] and other charts, when a song had sold one million singles there was an icon by the side of the title, a black dot or "bullet". [[platinum|Platinum]] sales which began in the mid seventies signified two million sales and had a triangle by their side. If a song was "number one with a bullet" it had achieved the best of the best so-to-speak, it had surpassed the coveted million sales mark ''and'' was on the top of the charts.

 
EDIT: This is a false definition.  The RIAA certifications of gold, platinum, etc. records are not affiliated with bullets.

===Adjective===
{{en-adj|-|pos=[[with]] a [[bullet]]}}

# {{context|of a hit record}} that has entered the [[chart]]s in a high position, or has climbed rapidly in the charts, or is thought to have the [[potential]] for further rapid advancement
#: ''"Free The World" has moved up again and is now Number 25 '''with a bullet''' on the Billboard Hot Dance/Club Play Charts!!"''

